    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B of a rectangular prism with dimensions 10 inches by 20 inches by 10 inches, we first need to find the actual lengths of the sides that make up the edges of a rectangular prism with these dimensions.\n\nThe diagonal in this rectangular prism will be going through a 2x2x2 triangular prism, along with the 10-inch dimension on each side. So, the length of the diagonal (of the rectangular prism) will be the square root of the sum of the square of the length of the 10-inch and 20-inch side lengths and the square of one of the diagonals of a 2x2x2 triangular prism.\n\nWe will call the length of the diagonal $l$.\n$l = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{20}{2}\\right)^2 + 10^2 + \\left(\\frac{10}{2}\\right)^2}$\n$l = \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2 + 5^2}$\n$l = \\sqrt{100 + 100 + 25}$\n$l = \\sqrt{4 = 125}$\n$l = \\sqrt{125}$\n$l = \\boxed{5\\sqrt{5}}$",
